Installation:

  - Check which NAND the device has by using the following procedure with stock firmware:
  - Go to to ''<nowiki>http://192.168.178.1/support.lua</nowiki>'' download the support data file and search for string "NAND device" to get the manufacturer kernel output.
  - Use Micron image if Micron is displayed otherwise the non-Micron image.
  - Use the [[https://raw.githubusercontent.com/openwrt/openwrt/master/scripts/flashing/eva_ramboot.py|eva_ramboot.py]] script to boot the initramfs image. The FTP port is only available for a short time while the device boots, during which the initrd image must be pushed with the linked script. Invoke it with ''eva_ramboot.py 192.168.178.1 <initrd>'', where ''<initrd>'' is the full path to the downloaded initrd image.
  - The device will now reboot, and the power LED will flash rapidly for a short time. It boots into a temporary OS which will allow you to upload the actual OpenWRT image.
  - Change your LAN adapter's IP to 192.168.1.10. You should be able to ''ping 192.168.1.1'', and port 22 should be open. Verify the connection with ''ssh root@192.168.1.1''. No password is needed.
  - Upload the OpenWRT image with ''ssh root@192.168.1.1 'cat > openwrt.bin' < <squashfs>'', where ''<squashfs>'' is the full path to the OpenWRT image appropriate for your device.
  - Connect to the box with ''ssh root@192.168.1.1'' and run ''ls''. You should be able to see ''openwrt.bin''. Validate it by running ''sha256sum openwrt.bin'' and comparing to the output of the same command on your local system, using the full path to the OpenWRT image.
  - Flash the OpenWRT image with ''sysupgrade -n openwrt.bin''. The box will reboot again, and the Info LED will flash red. Shortly after, it will boot into the newly installed OpenWRT.
  - The development snapshot image does not include the web interface, so you will only be able to connect via SSH. In order to  install LuCI, refer to [[:docs:guide-user:luci:luci.essentials]]
  - Since WiFi is not available on this hardware, you will need to set up an internet connection in order to install additional packages. You can either do so by setting up your current system as a router, or for example by connecting a cable to a different port. For the latter, assuming that the Internet connection is available on port 4, run ''vi /etc/config/network'', remove ''list ports 'lan4''' from the ''br-lan'' device, and add a new ''lan2'' interface analogous to the ''config interface 'lan''' section, using ''option device 'lan4''' device there instead of ''br-lan''. Alternatively, just set ''proto'' to ''dhcp'' if available. Exit ''vi'' and run ''service network restart''. After a short delay, you should have a prompt again, and ''ip addr'' should now show an IP address on the ''lan4'' interface.
  - If desired, you can now run ''apk add luci'' in order to install the web interface and connect to ''<nowiki>http://192.168.1.1</nowiki>'' in your browser.
  - For making USB work, an renesas xhci firmware file (e.g. v2026) is needed and it should be copied to /lib/firmware/ (file name renesas_usb_fw.mem).

→ [[docs:guide-user:installation:generic.flashing|Install OpenWrt]]


